  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract activity in New York totals $8.6B in FY2026 to date. The federal government made 266,057 awards in New York with an average award size of $32.2K. Obligations fell 28.9% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $4.6B in obligations. BROOKHAVEN SCIENCE ASSOCIATES LLC is the largest contractor with $842M in obligations. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) is the largest industry with $843M in obligations. Recent fiscal years show a steady decline from FY21 and FY22 peaks of $30.8B and $29.4B, respectively, down to $12.1B in FY25 and $8.6B in FY26. Energy, veterans, and civilian agency spending also appear in the top buyers list: the Department of Energy obligated $832M and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $707M. Aircraft and communications manufacturing sectors account for several of the top industry categories. These figures cover contract obligations and reflect partial totals for the current fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in New York federal contract spending year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 28.9% year over year. Fiscal-year totals declined from $30.8B in FY21 and $29.4B in FY22 to $16.5B in FY23, $15.1B in FY24, $12.1B in FY25, and $8.6B in FY26. The Department of Defense remains the largest buyer with $4.6B, which shapes the state total. Industry concentrations in research and aircraft-related manufacturing continue to hold large shares of obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and suppliers in New York now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $4.6B in FY2026 to date. The top buyers after Defense are the Department of Energy with $832M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $707M. BROOKHAVEN SCIENCE ASSOCIATES LLC received $842M, followed by LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION with $584M and L3HARRIS GLOBAL COMMUNICATIONS, INC. with $456M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) received $843M and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing received $802M."
      }
    ]
